In this lesson we'll look at something called sampling variation. Imagine you grab two random samples from the same population — say, two groups of Year 8 students from the same cohort. You'd expect them to give you basically the same results, right? Well, almost never. One class might have an average height of 164 centimetres, the other 166. One sample might have 48 percent preferring Brand X, another 52 percent. Same population, different samples, different numbers. And that's totally normal. So what does sampling variation look like? Each random sample is just a different mix of people, so the statistics you calculate from it will differ from sample to sample by chance. It's not a mistake, it's just how randomness works. Two key things to remember. First, random samples always vary a bit from each other. Second, the bigger your sample size, the smaller that variation gets. Also, remember that a sample statistic is only an estimate of the true population value — it's not the value itself. So always quote your sample size alongside any statistic you report, and if two samples disagree, it might just be sampling variation rather than a real difference. Now let's see it in action.